An empty slice is handled before reading or removing the root. The slice stores `n` values, so space usage is `O(n)`. Moving a value up or down takes `O(log n)`, while reading the root and size takes `O(1)`.

This version stores the heap in an `ArrayList`. The repair functions are recursive, which keeps the insert and remove logic fairly short. When a key changes, comparing it with its parent tells us whether it should go up or down.

The input index is treated as zero-based, matching the array representation.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
    private final ArrayList<Integer> heap = new ArrayList<>();

    private void moveUp(int index) {
        if (index == 0) {
            return;
        }

        int parent = (index - 1) / 2;
        if (heap.get(parent) >= heap.get(index)) {
            return;
        }

        int temp = heap.get(parent);
        heap.set(parent, heap.get(index));
        heap.set(index, temp);
        moveUp(parent);
    }

    private void moveDown(int index) {
        int left = index * 2 + 1;
        int right = index * 2 + 2;
        int largest = index;

        if (left < heap.size() && heap.get(left) > heap.get(largest)) {
            largest = left;
        }
        if (right < heap.size() && heap.get(right) > heap.get(largest)) {
            largest = right;
        }

        if (largest == index) {
            return;
        }

        int temp = heap.get(index);
        heap.set(index, heap.get(largest));
        heap.set(largest, temp);
        moveDown(largest);
    }

    public void initializeHeap() {
        heap.clear();
    }

    public void insert(int key) {
        heap.add(key);
        moveUp(heap.size() - 1);
    }

    public void changeKey(int index, int newVal) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= heap.size()) {
            return;
        }

        heap.set(index, newVal);
        if (index > 0 && heap.get(index) > heap.get((index - 1) / 2)) {
            moveUp(index);
        } else {
            moveDown(index);
        }
    }

    public void extractMax() {
        if (heap.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        int last = heap.size() - 1;
        heap.set(0, heap.get(last));
        heap.remove(last);
        if (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            moveDown(0);
        }
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return heap.isEmpty();
    }

    public int getMax() {
        return heap.isEmpty() ? -1 : heap.get(0);
    }

    public int heapSize() {
        return heap.size();
    }
}
```

The empty heap checks make `extractMax` safe when there is nothing to remove, and `getMax` returns `-1` in that case. Every repair can visit the height of the heap, so the changing operations take `O(log n)` time. The stored values need `O(n)` space.

This uses a `List<int>` and two small loops for restoring the heap property. The largest element always stays at position zero. `extractMax` does not return the removed value because the required platform method has a `void` return type.

### Dry run
If the heap is `[30, 18, 12, 5]`, extracting the maximum moves `5` to the root. It then swaps with `18`, leaving `[18, 5, 12]`.

The code also leaves the input state safe for an empty heap: `getMax` returns `-1`, while `extractMax` simply returns.

